{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeightVanishing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeightVanishing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.Young.YoungDiagram","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.GroupWithZero.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.GroupWithZero.Unbundled.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Units.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":18,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_eq_two_of_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_eq_two_of_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":363,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L363-L379","doc":"**Convention D pins the level to the integer `r = 2`.**\n\nGiven that a trace's Markov parameter is Wenzl's at level `r`, and that the\nparameter is convention D's (`Q = q²`, `z = q·z_D`, the factor `q` being the\nHecke-normalisation shift of `wenzlLevel_conventionD`), the level *is* `2`.\n\nUnconditional: this is `wenzlLevel_conventionD`'s algebra plus\n`level_unique_of_markovParameter`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_unique_of_markovParameter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_unique_of_markovParameter","kind":"theorem","line":336,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L336-L361","doc":"**`Q^r` is determined by the Markov parameter, and hence so is `r`.**\n\nIf two levels `r` and `s` sit at the same parameter `z` then `r = s`, for `Q`\nnot a root of unity. So \"which Wenzl level is this trace at?\" has at most one\nanswer — which is what lets `level_eq_two_of_conventionD` below turn convention\nD's parameter into the *integer* 2 rather than merely into `Q^r = Q²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.pow_level_mul_eq_of_markovParameter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.pow_level_mul_eq_of_markovParameter","kind":"theorem","line":327,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L327-L334","doc":"Wenzl's parameter relation, solved for `Q^r`: `Q^r(1 − Q + z) = z`.\n\nPure algebra — no hypothesis at all, not even `Q ≠ 0`. This is the inversion\n`Q^r = z/(1 − Q + z)` quoted in the file header, in the form that avoids\ndividing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.discriminates_at_n_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.discriminates_at_n_three","kind":"theorem","line":289,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L289-L305","doc":"**The criterion discriminates.** At `n = 3` over `ℚ` with `Q = 2`, the\nthree-row diagram `(1³)` has weight zero and the two-row diagram `(2,1)` does\nnot. These are the two blocks PR #5185 measured — `y₍₁³₎ = 0`,\n`y₍₂,₁₎ = q²/(q²+1)² ≠ 0` — so the Lean returns the measured verdict on the\nmeasured case, and it is not returning \"zero\" for everything. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_two_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_two_one","kind":"theorem","line":282,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L282-L287","doc":"`(2,1)` — two rows — has `colLen 0 = 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_one_one_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_ofRowLens_one_one_one","kind":"theorem","line":275,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L275-L280","doc":"`(1,1,1)` — three rows — has `colLen 0 = 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.not_root_of_unity_of_pos_ne_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.not_root_of_unity_of_pos_ne_one","kind":"theorem","line":265,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L265-L273","doc":"**The genericity hypothesis holds at every positive `Q ≠ 1`**, hence in\nparticular at the substrate `Q = q₀²` (`q₀ ≈ 1.10998`, so `Q ≈ 1.232`). This\nis the archimedean instantiation: it lives after the substrate→archimedean\nwall and is not used by any statement above it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_ne_zero_of_two_rows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_ne_zero_of_two_rows","kind":"theorem","line":247,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L247-L254","doc":"The surviving side, stated positively: a diagram with at most two rows has\nnonzero weight. This is the half that makes the criterion sharp — without it\n\"the `≥ 3`-row blocks die\" would be compatible with everything dying. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff_rowLens","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff_rowLens","kind":"theorem","line":239,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L239-L245","doc":"The same statement with \"at least three rows\" spelled as the length of the\nrow-length list, which is how `λ`'s number of rows is usually written. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.conventionD_weight_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L214-L237","doc":"**The all-`n` Temperley–Lieb weight-vanishing lemma, at the level of the\nformula.**\n\nAt convention D — equivalently, by `wenzlLevel_conventionD`, at Wenzl level\n`r = 2` — the normalised level-2 principal specialisation of `λ` vanishes\n**iff** `λ` has at least three rows. `Z` is the normaliser `Σ_μ f^μ s_μ`\nenforcing `Σ_λ f^λ y_λ = tr(1) = 1`; only its non-vanishing is used, so the\nstatement is independent of which normalisation convention is in force.\n\n**Unconditional, and about `schurPrincipal` — not about the Ocneanu weight.**\nThis says nothing about the Markov trace on `H_n(Q)`; it is a fact about the\nhook-content product. The corresponding statement about the actual weight is\n`SchurWeyl.ocneanuWeight_conventionD_eq_zero_iff`, unconditional (see *The weights\nthemselves* below).\n\nThis is the statement PR #5207 named. It holds for every `n` and every `λ`,\nand it is the reason `radical = ⟨E⁽³⁾⟩`: the blocks the trace annihilates are\nexactly the `≥ 3`-row ones, whose dimensions sum to `n! − Catalan(n)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L164-L210","doc":"**The vanishing criterion, for every `n`, every `λ` and every level `r`.**\n\nThe level-`r` principal specialisation vanishes exactly when `λ` has more than\n`r` rows:\n\n`s_λ(1, Q, …, Q^{r−1}) = 0 ⟺ ℓ(λ) > r`.\n\n`Q` is assumed not to be a root of unity — precisely the genericity under\nwhich `H_n(Q)` is semisimple and the weights are defined. Without it the\nstatement is false, so the hypothesis is doing real work rather than\ndecorating the signature: at a root of unity a *hook length* can also kill a\ndenominator and further numerator factors can vanish off content `−r`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_gt_of_content_eq_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.colLen_gt_of_content_eq_neg","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L153-L162","doc":"A cell of `μ` of content `−r` forces `μ` to contain `(r, 0)`, hence to have\nmore than `r` rows. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.wenzlLevel_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.wenzlLevel_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L132-L149","doc":"**Convention D is Wenzl level `r = 2`.**\n\nWenzl's parameter relation `z_W = Q^r(1−Q)/(1−Q^r)` inverts to\n`Q^r = z_W/(1 − Q + z_W)`. Convention D sits at `Q = q²` and `z_W = q·z_D`\n(the factor `q` is the Hecke-normalisation shift between\n`g² = (Q−1)g + Q` and `σ² = (q − q⁻¹)σ + 1`, under which `σ = g/q` and\n`Q = q²`). Substituting `z_D = q³/(q²+1)` gives `Q^r = q⁴ = Q²`, so `r = 2`\non the nose — the SU(2) level, whose row cap is the Temperley–Lieb one.\n\nNot cited: this is an algebraic identity in `q`, so it is proved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal","kind":"def","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L107-L130","doc":"The **level-`r` principal specialisation** of the Schur function, in the\nhook-content product form used throughout the corpus:\n\n`s_λ(1, Q, …, Q^{r−1}) = Q^{n(λ)} · ∏_{x ∈ λ} (1 − Q^{r + c(x)}) / (1 − Q^{h(x)})`\n\nThis is `markov_weights_symbolic.schur_principal_symbolic` and\n`schur_principal_mpmath._schur_principal_with`, transcribed verbatim.\n\nWenzl's theorem — the external input, and the only one — is that these *are*\nthe Ocneanu/Markov trace weights on `H_n(Q)` at Markov parameter\n`z_W = Q^r(1−Q)/(1−Q^r)`, up to the normalisation `Σ_λ f^λ y_λ = 1`.\n-- Ref: [wenzl1988] https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01404457\n   Wenzl, \"Hecke algebras of type $A_n$ and subfactors\", Invent. Math. 92\n   (1988) 349–383 — the source of the weight formula.\n-- Ref: [orellana1998] https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9806163\n   Orellana, \"Weights of Markov Traces on Hecke Algebras\", p. 17, restates\n   Wenzl's type-A result in exactly this form: *\"In [W1] Wenzl showed that\n   the weights of the Markov trace (with parameter `z = q^r(1−q)/(1−q^r)`)\n   on the Hecke algebra of type A are given by the symmetric Schur functions\n   `s_{λ,r}(q)`.\"* **Caution:** Orellana's \"type D\" is the *Coxeter* type\n   `D_n` and has nothing to do with this repo's trace convention D. Only the\n   type-A statement above is used. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.one_le_hook","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.one_le_hook","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L93-L101","doc":"Every cell of a diagram has hook length at least `1`: its arm and its leg\nare each at least `0`, and the `+1` counts the cell itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.nPart","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.nPart","kind":"def","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L89-L91","doc":"`n(λ) = Σ_i (i−1)·λ_i`, i.e. the sum of the 0-indexed row indices over the\ncells. Matches `markov_weights_symbolic.n_partition`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.hook","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.hook","kind":"def","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L84-L87","doc":"The **hook length** `h(i, j) = arm + leg + 1` of a cell of `μ`. Matches\n`computations/markov/markov_weights_symbolic.hooks_and_contents`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.content","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.WenzlWeight.content","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/WenzlWeightVanishing.lean#L78-L82","doc":"The **content** of a cell, `c(i, j) = j − i`, as an integer (cells are\n0-indexed, matching `YoungDiagram`). Contents in the first column are\n`0, −1, −2, …`, so a cell of content `−r` exists exactly when the diagram has\nmore than `r` rows — the fact the whole file turns on. ","signatureChunk":0}]}